<header><p class="enc-kicker">Person</p><h1 class="enc-title">Jerome</h1><p class="enc-meta"><span class="dates">c. 347–<a href="/years/420">420</a></span> · <a href="/centuries/4th-century">4th century</a> · Also known as Saint Jerome, Eusebius Hieronymus</p></header>
<div class="enc-body"><p class="enc-desc">Jerome was a Christian priest, scholar and Doctor of the Church, born in <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/dalmatia">Dalmatia</a>, who spent his later life as a monk in <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/bethlehem">Bethlehem</a>. His <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/latin">Latin</a> translation of the <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/bible">Bible</a>, later known as the Vulgate, became the standard text of the Western Church for over a thousand years. He also wrote biblical commentaries, letters and a continuation of Eusebius' chronicle.</p><figure class="enc-fig"><img src="/images/encyclopedia/jerome.jpg" alt="Jerome" width="320" height="390" fetchpriority="high"><figcaption>Image: Matthias Stom · Public domain · <a href="https://commons.wikimedia.org/wiki/File:MatthiasStom-SaintJerome-Nantes.jpg" target="_blank" rel="noopener">Wikimedia Commons</a></figcaption></figure></div>
<section class="enc-more"><h2 class="enc-more-h">Early life and background</h2><p>Eusebius Sophronius Hieronymus was born at Stridon and was of Illyrian ancestry. He went to <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/rome">Rome</a> with his friend Bonosus of <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/serdica">Sardica</a> to study rhetoric and philosophy, learned <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/latin">Latin</a> under the philologist Aelius Donatus, and was baptised there. He described the guilt that followed the escapades of his student days, and how he visited the tombs of the martyrs in the catacombs on Sundays. Drawn to ascetic penance, he went for a time to the desert of <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/chalcis">Chalcis</a>, south-east of <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/antioch">Antioch</a>, known as the Syrian Thebaid for its many hermits. There he began to learn <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/hebrew">Hebrew</a> under a converted Jew.</p><h2 class="enc-more-h">Career</h2><p>As a protégé of Pope Damasus I, Jerome revised the Vetus Latina Gospels in <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/rome">Rome</a> against Greek manuscripts. He gathered around him well-born women, among them the widows Marcella and Paula and Paula's daughters Blaesilla and Eustochium, who turned towards the monastic life. His unsparing criticism of the Roman clergy brought hostility, and after Damasus died on 10 December 384 he was forced to leave Rome after an inquiry into allegations of an improper relationship with Paula. Paula's money later paid for his stay in a monastery at <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/bethlehem">Bethlehem</a>, next to the Church of the Nativity, where he completed his translation of the <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/bible">Bible</a>. He died on 30 September 420.</p><h2 class="enc-more-h">Works and ideas</h2><p>Jerome began in 382 by correcting the Vetus Latina New Testament. By 390 he was translating the <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/hebrew-bible">Hebrew Bible</a> from <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/hebrew">Hebrew</a>, and he finished by 405. Earlier <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/latin">Latin</a> versions of the <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/old-testament">Old Testament</a> rested on the <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/septuagint">Septuagint</a>, and his choice of a Hebrew text went against the advice of most Christians, among them Augustine, who thought the Septuagint inspired. The result became known as the Vulgate, and in 1546 the <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/council-of-trent">Council of Trent</a> declared it authoritative. From 405 to 420 he wrote commentaries on Scripture.</p><p>His other writings include the Chronicon, a translation and continuation of the chronicle of Eusebius, and On Illustrious Men, a biobibliography of Christian writers written in 392 and 393. His letters give a vivid picture of the age. After Augustine, he is the most voluminous writer of ancient Latin Christianity, and the <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/catholic-church">Catholic Church</a> counts him among the four Great Latin Church Fathers and as patron saint of translators, librarians and encyclopedists. Artists often show him with a lion.</p><p class="enc-more-src">This account follows the Wikipedia article “Jerome” (CC BY-SA 4.0), linked under Read further below.</p></section>
<section class="enc-quotes"><h2 class="enc-section-title">Across the Institute</h2><ul><li><blockquote>Early versions appeared in Latin, Syriac and Coptic; <b>Jerome</b>’s Latin Vulgate became the Bible of the medieval West; the missionaries Cyril and Methodius created a Slavonic script in the 9th century to translate the scriptures; and the Reformation produced vernacular Bibles such as Luther’s German Bible and the…</blockquote><a href="/religion/christianity">Christianity · Texts &amp; Teachers</a></li><li><blockquote>Justin Martyr, about 145, charged the cult with imitating the Christian communion, <b>Jerome</b> lists the seven grades, and Tertullian and Firmicus Maternus attacked the cult.</blockquote><a href="/religion/mithraism">Mithraism · Texts &amp; Teachers</a></li><li><blockquote>Tertullian, around 200, was among the first Christian writers to use it, and <b>Jerome</b>’s translation of the Bible, the Vulgate, completed in the early 5th century, became the standard text of Western Christianity for more than a thousand years.</blockquote><a href="/language/latin">Latin · Speakers &amp; Spread</a></li><li><blockquote>…later named Galatia after them; the victories of Attalus I of Pergamon over the Galatians were celebrated with statues such as the Dying Gaul, and in the 4th century CE <b>Saint Jerome</b> wrote that the Galatians still spoke a language close to that of the Treveri of Gaul.</blockquote><a href="/culture/celts">Celts · Contact &amp; Exchange</a></li><li><blockquote>It belongs to the early Latin translations of scripture known as the Vetus Latina: the Vulgate was commissioned only in 382, and the hymn's Latin wording for &quot;the highest&quot; differs from the one <b>Jerome</b> later preferred.</blockquote><a href="/songs/gloria-in-excelsis-deo">Gloria in excelsis Deo</a></li><li><blockquote><b>Jerome</b> is associated with three Latin psalters, and in England the first Book of Common Prayer used Myles Coverdale's prose rendering.</blockquote><a href="/songs/psalm-23">Psalm 23</a></li></ul></section>
<section class="enc-where enc-born"><h2 class="enc-section-title">Born and died</h2><p class="enc-born-p">Died in <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/bethlehem">Bethlehem</a>, in what is now <a href="/countries/palestine">Palestine</a>. <span class="enc-born-src">Places as Wikidata records them.</span></p><figure class="st-locator enc-loc"><a href="/atlas?at=31.7,35.21&amp;year=394" aria-label="Bethlehem on the atlas"><svg class="map map-loc" viewBox="5457 1009 900 540" role="img" aria-label="Map: the place of death of Jerome"><image href="/maps/world.svg" x="0" y="0" width="10000" height="4393"/><path class="map-dot-halo" d="M5907 1279h0"/><path class="map-dot-me" d="M5907 1279h0"/></svg></a><figcaption>The dot marks the place of death. <a href="/atlas?at=31.7,35.21&amp;year=394">What was here in 394 CE? &rarr;</a></figcaption></figure></section>
<section class="enc-words" id="in-their-words"><h2 class="enc-section-title">In their words</h2><blockquote class="enc-q"><p>&#8220;My voice sticks in my throat; and, as I dictate, sobs choke my utterance. The City which had taken the whole world was itself taken…&#8221;</p><footer>Letter 127, to Principia, 412 &#183; tr. W. H. Fremantle, 1893 &#183; on the sack of Rome by the Goths in 410 &#183; <a href="https://www.newadvent.org/fathers/3001127.htm" rel="noopener">www.newadvent.org</a></footer></blockquote><blockquote class="enc-q"><p>&#8220;Asked who and what I was I replied: I am a Christian. But He who presided said: You lie. You are a follower of Cicero and not of Christ.&#8221;</p><footer>Letter 22, to Eustochium, 384 &#183; tr. W. H. Fremantle, 1893 &#183; a dream of his youth, in which he stood before the judgement seat; its Judge speaks the last two sentences &#183; <a href="https://www.newadvent.org/fathers/3001022.htm" rel="noopener">www.newadvent.org</a></footer></blockquote><blockquote class="enc-q"><p>&#8220;Let her treasures be not silks or gems but manuscripts of the holy scriptures; and in these let her think less of gilding, and Babylonian parchment, and arabesque patterns, than of correctness and accurate punctuation.&#8221;</p><footer>Letter 107, to Laeta, on the education of her daughter, 403 &#183; tr. W. H. Fremantle, 1893 &#183; <a href="https://www.newadvent.org/fathers/3001107.htm" rel="noopener">www.newadvent.org</a></footer></blockquote><p class="enc-article-foot"><a class="enc-random-link" href="/encyclopedia/quotations">Quotations from 595 people &rarr;</a></p></section>
